  cta={{ href: '/2048/', label: 'Play 2048' }}
>
  <p>
    2048 looks like a game of luck — tiles spawn at random, the board fills up, and one bad
    swipe ends a run that felt like it was going well. But the players who hit 2048 again and
    again aren't luckier; they follow a handful of rules that turn the random spawns into
    something predictable. This guide explains that method and, just as importantly,
    <em>why</em> each rule works. The fastest way to follow along is to
    <a href="/2048/">play a round</a> while you read.
  </p>

  <h2>How 2048 works</h2>
  <p>
    Every move, you slide <strong>all</strong> the tiles in one of four directions — up, down,
    left or right. When two tiles with the same number collide, they <strong>merge</strong> into
    a single tile worth their sum: two 2s become a 4, two 4s become an 8, and so on up the chain.
    After each move a new tile (usually a 2, occasionally a 4) <strong>spawns</strong> on a random
    empty square. You win when you build the <strong>2048</strong> tile — and you can keep going
    for higher tiles afterwards. You lose only when the board is completely full and no merge is
    possible in any direction.
  </p>

  <h2>The strategy that wins games</h2>

  <h3>1. Pick a corner and never let your biggest tile leave it</h3>
  <p>
    This is the single most important habit. Choose one corner — most strong players use
    <strong>bottom-right or bottom-left</strong> — and treat it as the permanent home for your
    largest tile. Why a corner? A tile in the middle of the board can be pushed in four
    directions and constantly gets separated from the tiles it needs to merge with. A tile in a
    corner only moves when you swipe toward the two walls it's already touching, so it stays put
    while everything else organises around it.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Build a monotonic chain — the "snake"</h3>
  <p>
    Keep your tiles in <strong>decreasing order</strong> along the row or column that leads into
    your anchor corner. Imagine a snake winding back and forth: the biggest tile in the corner,
    the next-biggest beside it, then the next, all the way down to your small new tiles. When the
    values descend in order, a merge at one end <strong>cascades</strong> — your 256 merges into
    your 512, freeing the space that lets the next pair combine, and the whole row collapses
    upward into the big tile. A jumbled board can't do that.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Favour two directions and avoid the move that frees your anchor</h3>
  <p>
    If your anchor is in the bottom-right, build using mostly <strong>down and right</strong>.
    Those two swipes keep pressing everything toward the corner and maintaining the snake. The
    move you must guard against is the opposite one — never swipe <strong>up</strong> when your
    anchor sits on the bottom unless you have genuinely no other legal move. A single careless
    "up" can rip your largest tile out of its corner and undo the whole structure in one swipe.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Keep your anchor row full</h3>
  <p>
    Try to keep the bottom row (or whichever row holds your anchor) <strong>completely filled</strong>.
    An empty square in that row is an invitation for a stray 2 to spawn <em>underneath</em> your
    big tiles, wedging a useless small value into the structure that you then have to dig out.
    A full anchor row also means a downward swipe doesn't disturb it — your foundation stays
    locked while you work the rows above.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Plan merges before the board gets tight</h3>
  <p>
    Don't swipe on autopilot. Look at the board and think about which row is about to collapse
    into the next size up, and which swipe sets that up without breaking the snake. The time to
    think is <em>before</em> you're down to one or two empty squares — once the board is crowded,
    your choices vanish and you're forced into the move you've been avoiding. A little planning
    early keeps you out of that corner (the bad kind).
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes that end runs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Swiping all four directions at random</strong> — the fastest way to scatter your
      tiles and lose the snake.</li>
    <li><strong>Moving the anchor out of its corner</strong> — one stray "up" can cost you the
      whole game.</li>
    <li><strong>Chasing small merges in the wrong direction</strong>, pulling tiles away from the
      corner just to combine a couple of 4s.</li>
    <li><strong>Filling the board with no plan</strong>, leaving mismatched tiles that can never
      merge.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting undo exists</strong> — on CardHearth you can take back a bad swipe, so
      a single mistake doesn't have to end the run.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Practise the right way</h2>
  <p>
    CardHearth gives every board a number, so you can <strong>replay the exact same start</strong>
    to test a different line or share it with a friend to see who climbs higher. Use
    <em>Undo</em> to take back a swipe that broke your snake and learn from it — that's how the
    corner-and-snake method becomes second nature far faster than grinding random boards.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

